{"title":"2021 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(No_Logarithm)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (No Logarithm)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Logarithmic_Tricks)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Logarithmic Tricks)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_hippopotamus1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by hippopotamus1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_The_Power_of_Logic\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by The Power of Logic</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the sum of all positive real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> for which<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91d43f026978eb57bfbe0d83263ca432924008bf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}=\\sqrt2^{2^x}.\\]\" width=\"100\" height=\"23\" />Which of the following statements is true?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/1/871d7278b68025059fb8a10e557456b2c44e2425.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }S&lt;\\sqrt2 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) }S=\\sqrt2 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) }\\sqrt2&lt;S&lt;2\\qquad \\textbf{(D) }2\\le S&lt;6 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) }S\\ge 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"647\" height=\"21\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/7/3771a5954fe4691038b4d2f98242bba1e045703d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} x^{2^{\\sqrt{2}}} &amp;= {\\sqrt{2}}^{2^x} \\\\ 2^{\\sqrt{2}} \\log_2 x &amp;= 2^{x} \\log_2 \\sqrt{2}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"181\" height=\"57\" />\n(At this point we see by inspection that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db1f87c7de444f61b4aa1184aeae8678599199c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> is a solution.)\n</p><p>We simplify the RHS, then take the base-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> logarithm for both sides:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/6/0f6c7e025b2f79d77705348577395144fd2818d8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 2^{\\sqrt{2}} \\log_2 x &amp;= 2^{x-1} \\\\ \\log_2{\\left(2^{\\sqrt{2}} \\log_2 x\\right)} &amp;= x-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} + \\log_2 \\log_2 x &amp;= x-1 \\\\ \\log_2 \\log_2 x &amp;= x - 1 - \\sqrt{2}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"245\" height=\"121\" />\nThe RHS is a line; the LHS is a concave curve that looks like a logarithm and has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> intercept at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/e/41ea8e274952593798f1b2f6bb5632d785d75d59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,0).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>There are at most two solutions, one of which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb485bd3ce593662cabace2375882fc28b737206.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> But note that at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/b/5bb4b0e681c5981e9358f6b413cc4f362c8f3e9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"47\" height=\"15\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/4/9444c60974a1eddf1c528fd5bc12a0d616ade810.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_2 \\log_2 {2} = 0 &gt; 2 - 1 - \\sqrt{2},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"228\" height=\"24\" /> meaning that the log log curve is above the line, so it must intersect the line again at a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/b/82b8cbd199de3ab8bffcd1cdf16e211d10a50979.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"47\" height=\"12\" /> Now we check <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> and see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/8/0780418cf73279e9c6404cb23b7d8234168b9485.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_2 \\log_2 {4} = 1 &lt; 4 - 1 - \\sqrt{2},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"228\" height=\"24\" /> which means at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> the line is already above the log log curve. Thus, the second solution lies in the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/0/ea0b737090b1b7c2daf0a218b56c4bd66e7fcc57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,4).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />\nThe answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/986bf254c71341f7e5908dc7d3543e8fb9fac140.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }2\\le S&lt;6}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"129\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>~ccx09\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We rewrite the right side without using square roots, then take the base-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> logarithm for both sides:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/a/cda436957a2d933de2590cd7a7ac35911099a5f6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}&amp;=\\left(2^\\frac12\\right)^{2^x} \\\\ x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}&amp;=2^{\\frac12\\cdot2^x} \\\\ x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}&amp;=2^{2^{x-1}} \\\\ \\log_2{\\left(x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}\\right)}&amp;=\\log_2{\\left(2^{2^{x-1}}\\right)} \\\\ 2^{\\sqrt2}\\log_2{x}&amp;=2^{x-1}. \\hspace{20mm} (*) \\end{align*}\" width=\"267\" height=\"170\" />\nBy observations, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/2/012da1f8af6acd7279033e596baa362d800b3847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> is one solution. Graphing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/5/815c6e847de9e9aa065f9ecc5b855a2eb6c8ca31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)=2^{\\sqrt2}\\log_2{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"22\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/2/4221acfa0a740604a1ff52c9e92b9cb202eacdb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(x)=2^{x-1},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"96\" height=\"19\" /> we conclude that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/0/9d02e715e7f7195675151570530c5b62ba7b2744.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(*)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> has two solutions, with the smaller solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/b/70b45c5ac650a362014fddf817e52abf12d97c40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> We construct the following table of values: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/0/9c0b78183ab2268b7cc036a5f7eb0162a5543f4a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c} &amp; &amp; &amp; \\\\ [-2ex] \\boldsymbol{x} &amp; \\boldsymbol{f(x)=2^{\\sqrt2}\\log_2{x}} &amp; \\boldsymbol{g(x)=2^{x-1}} &amp; \\textbf{Comparison} \\\\ [1ex] \\hline &amp; &amp; &amp; \\\\ [-1ex] 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; \\\\ [1ex] \\sqrt2 &amp; 2^{\\sqrt2-1} &amp; 2^{\\sqrt2-1} &amp; f\\left(\\sqrt2\\right)=g\\left(\\sqrt2\\right) \\\\ [1ex] 2 &amp; 2^{\\sqrt2} &amp; 2 &amp; f(2)&gt;g(2) \\\\ [1ex] 3 &amp; 2^{\\sqrt2}\\log_2{3} &amp; 2^2 &amp; \\\\ [1ex] 4 &amp; 2^{\\sqrt2+1} &amp; 2^3 &amp; f(4)&lt;g(4) \\\\ [1ex] \\end{array}\\]\" width=\"492\" height=\"202\" />\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/d/1bd2b4e8b24b5dfd4a500292672af493746ef676.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=t$\" width=\"40\" height=\"11\" /> be the larger solution. Since exponential functions outgrow logarithmic functions, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/e/41e960ad1182efadffcbf000fdaac71d187f8bb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)&lt;g(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/8/22825c2f2f5aee7d9be14ed737c503b30e9d2d23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;t.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> By the <b>Intermediate Value Theorem</b>, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/c/22caa78c07b26f0c7dc431f5abad15e722a03bd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t\\in(2,4),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" /> from which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/b/2bb3709bf7ef1c2e8d09956a64cb5589d05b077b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S=\\sqrt2+t\\in\\left(\\sqrt2+2,\\sqrt2+4\\right).\\]\" width=\"258\" height=\"32\" />\nFinally, approximating with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/c/90c62662dd1fb1519a97ed5db06b6f5e49922099.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt2\\approx1.414$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> results in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/986bf254c71341f7e5908dc7d3543e8fb9fac140.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }2\\le S&lt;6}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"129\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>The graphs of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/e/97eded22ccc3e54189a8f157c4e81f36fe034a26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/7/b07623f87e4369fb18ed7ca64337e70c2ee0e5d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=g(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> are shown below:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79bfab877c3bdfd878127d711fb0c96f3452aa00.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by MRENTHUSIASM */ size(1200,200);   int xMin = 0; int xMax = 5; int yMin = 0; int yMax = 5;  //Draws the horizontal gridlines void horizontalLines() {   for (int i = yMin+1; i &lt; yMax; ++i)   {     draw((xMin,i)--(xMax,i), mediumgray+linewidth(0.4));   } }  //Draws the vertical gridlines void verticalLines() {   for (int i = xMin+1; i &lt; xMax; ++i)   {     draw((i,yMin)--(i,yMax), mediumgray+linewidth(0.4));   } }  //Draws the horizontal ticks void horizontalTicks() {   for (int i = yMin+1; i &lt; yMax; ++i)   {     draw((-1/8,i)--(1/8,i), black+linewidth(1));   } }  //Draws the vertical ticks void verticalTicks() {   for (int i = xMin+1; i &lt; xMax; ++i)   {     draw((i,-1/8)--(i,1/8), black+linewidth(1));   } }  horizontalLines(); verticalLines(); horizontalTicks(); verticalTicks(); draw((xMin,0)--(xMax,0),black+linewidth(1.5),EndArrow(5)); draw((0,yMin)--(0,yMax),black+linewidth(1.5),EndArrow(5)); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(xMax,0),(2,0)); label(&quot;$y$&quot;,(0,yMax),(0,2));  real f(real x) {return 2^sqrt(2)*log(x)/log(2);}; real g(real x) {return 2^(x-1);};  draw(graph(f,1,3.65),red,&quot;$y=2^{\\sqrt2}\\log_2{x}$&quot;); draw(graph(g,0,3.32),blue,&quot;$y=2^{x-1}$&quot;);  pair A, B; A = intersectionpoint(graph(f,1,2),graph(g,1,2)); B = intersectionpoint(graph(f,2,4),graph(g,2,4)); dot(A,linewidth(4.5)); dot(B,linewidth(4.5));  label(&quot;$0$&quot;,(0,0),2.5*SW); label(&quot;$\\sqrt2$&quot;,(A.x,0),2.25*S); label(&quot;$t$&quot;,(B.x,0),3*S); label(&quot;$4$&quot;,(4,0),3*S); label(&quot;$4$&quot;,(0,4),3*W);  draw(A--(A.x,0),dashed); draw(B--(B.x,0),dashed);  add(legend(),point(E),40E,UnFill); [/asy]\" width=\"612\" height=\"335\" />\n~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28No_Logarithm.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(No_Logarithm)\">Solution 3 (No Logarithm)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (No Logarithm)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>While utilizing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/8/698c1c9cde3469a346091405ffbbcedf96609562.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"23\" height=\"16\" /> may seem conventional, graphing may also be used. Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/9/6e92475c0de86ae69b86ff730bf2b1b3e6f3d8c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"35\" height=\"22\" /> is a U-shaped, differentiable curve. Moreover, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2ba441d819a220ab58b5b7b5b0b8227f3a8576b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt2^{2^x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"25\" /> is an exponentially increasing function. Furthermore, we could notice that the two graphs meet at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db1f87c7de444f61b4aa1184aeae8678599199c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/a/0ea9bcd4ba51eecdac5d913dcf733f0c0232831b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}     x &amp; x^{2^{\\sqrt2}} &amp; \\sqrt2^{2^x} &amp; \\text{Comparison} \\\\     \\hline     0 &amp; 0 &amp; \\sqrt{2} &amp; x^{2^{\\sqrt2}} &lt; \\sqrt2^{2^x} \\\\     1 &amp; 2&lt;2^{\\sqrt{2}}&lt;4 &amp; 2 &amp; x^{2^{\\sqrt2}} &lt; \\sqrt2^{2^x} \\\\     \\sqrt{2} &amp; {\\sqrt{2}}^{2^{\\sqrt2}} &amp; {\\sqrt{2}}^{2^{\\sqrt2}} &amp; x^{2^{\\sqrt2}} = \\sqrt2^{2^x} \\\\     2 &amp; 4&lt;2^{2^{\\sqrt2}}&lt;16 &amp; 4 &amp; x^{2^{\\sqrt2}} &gt; \\sqrt2^{2^x} \\\\     3 &amp; 9&lt;3^{2^{\\sqrt2}}&lt;81 &amp; 16 &amp; NA \\\\     4 &amp; 16&lt;4^{2^{\\sqrt2}}&lt;256 &amp; 256 &amp; x^{2^{\\sqrt2}} &lt; \\sqrt2^{2^x} \\\\ \\end{array}\\]\" width=\"361\" height=\"192\" />\nBecause the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/4/cc4ed0e1bf1324013e08c840d0effa42ba5bc34c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2&lt;x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}&lt;x^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"119\" height=\"23\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2ba441d819a220ab58b5b7b5b0b8227f3a8576b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt2^{2^x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"25\" /> is an exponentially increasing function, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/9/6e92475c0de86ae69b86ff730bf2b1b3e6f3d8c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2^{\\sqrt2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"35\" height=\"22\" /> can never meet or catch up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2ba441d819a220ab58b5b7b5b0b8227f3a8576b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt2^{2^x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"25\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/4/324cf3130e0dfde7765a0f6a483f9ebc6b2466fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\ge4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" />. In another words, only two intersections, or the roots of the equation, exists. The roots are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4dc1409208ff6a3039f4871d85c16763334983.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" /> and a constant in the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/2/9f2b5e9f4836303a3b8631013d7820b947207f0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2&lt;r_2&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"17\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/5/c75c790339d1b7c6867cf793b298a4a97d35a7c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=\\sqrt{2}+r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"100\" height=\"22\" />. It is evident that the possible range for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/5/de52ffb6d2c0f8e06df3dd7b303875d1f668573b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }2\\le S&lt;6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"128\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~MaPhyCom\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_.28Logarithmic_Tricks.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Logarithmic_Tricks)\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Logarithmic Tricks)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Logarithmic Tricks)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/uCTpLB-kGR4\">https://youtu.be/uCTpLB-kGR4</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_hippopotamus1\">Video Solution by hippopotamus1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by hippopotamus1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GjO6C_qC13U&amp;feature=youtu.be\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GjO6C_qC13U&amp;feature=youtu.be</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_The_Power_of_Logic\">Video Solution by The Power of Logic</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by The Power of Logic\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/0i6qoGpk_Ew\">https://youtu.be/0i6qoGpk_Ew</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B\" title=\"2021 AMC 12B\">2021 AMC 12B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2021 AMC 12B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2021 AMC 12B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2021 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 20\">Problem 20</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=